\subsection{Research Loop}\label{sec:loop}
% [-P2-04 (approved 2026-11-10)-]{+P2-04:+} restructured in revision 6 by [-Claude: provisional,-]{+Claude; the+} author [-must review-]{+stated on 2026-11-15 that Part 2 is reviewed+} and [-rewrite.-]{+approved. Revision 7 changed names only (camera_ready/CHANGELOG_CAMERA_READY.md).+}
\humanauthor[P2-04 r6]{Each attempt follows the eight steps below. The agent acts in steps 2 and 8 and, [-historically,-]{+in original runs,+} labels the outcome in step 7. Protected code does everything else (Appendix~\ref{app:repro} names the functions).
{\small\begin{enumerate}\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}\setlength{\topsep}{2pt}
\item \emph{Controller:} show the agent this campaign's records, the current frontier and the parent's source.
\item \emph{Agent:} return a hypothesis, the intended change, a change category, the chosen parent and a patch to \texttt{train.py}.
\item \emph{Controller:} validate. The parent must be on the current frontier [-(historical)-]{+(original)+} or parent-eligible in the same arm [-(Pair-A).-]{+(extended).+} Protected files and the source must match their SHA-256 hashes, and only \texttt{train.py} may change.
\item \emph{Controller:} train under the contract, 300\,s or, in the [-Pair-A-]{+extended+} fixed-token arm, $N^\star$ counted tokens.
\item \emph{Controller:} verify the checkpoint, measure validation BPB, check that cached decoding matches a full recomputation, and measure decode throughput and peak allocated memory.
\item \emph{Controller:} append an immutable record of the hypothesis, patch, hashes, metrics and any failure text. A failure still uses up the attempt.
\item \emph{Controller and agent:} update the archive. [-Historically-]{+In the original experiments+} a completed candidate was retained unless another completed candidate or the registered same-seed baseline dominated it on raw BPB, decode speed and memory, with no noise margin. The agent labelled each outcome, and the runner rejected any label that disagreed with this rule.
\item \emph{Agent:} choose the next parent among the retained [-(historical)-]{+(original)+} or parent-eligible [-(Pair-A)-]{+(extended)+} records.
\end{enumerate}}
Without a noise margin the archive keeps noise-level trade-offs. In AP-Q's campaign A9 and A10 build the same model, yet A10 was retained. In [-Pair-A-]{+the extended experiments+} every quality-valid record was parent-eligible even when its speed was uncertain.}

\subsection{Human Interventions}\label{sec:interventions}
% [-P2-06 (approved 2026-11-12)-]{+P2-06:+} restructured in revision 6 by [-Claude: provisional,-]{+Claude; the+} author [-must review-]{+stated on 2026-11-15 that Part 2 is reviewed+} and [-rewrite.-]{+approved. Revision 7 changed names only (camera_ready/CHANGELOG_CAMERA_READY.md).+}
\humanauthor[P2-06 r6]{I chose the objectives, the 300-second contract, the matched-exposure design, the recipes for the panel, E1 and the Opus tests, the comparators and the interpretation. I made or approved each intervention below, and AI coding assistants carried out several of them at my instruction. None of them counts as autonomous agent research.

\textbf{(i) Seed-42 rollback.} Attempt 4 of the seed-42 AutoPareto campaign failed a cache-correctness check, was labelled dominated, and attempts 5--8 then ran on 2026-07-28. The check itself was faulty. After it was repaired I discarded attempts 5--8, because they had followed a frontier that wrongly excluded attempt 4 (ids 88--90 are absent). Attempt 4 was re-measured on a recreated checkpoint and became retained, and attempt 5 was replayed unchanged. Attempt 10 was re-adjudicated after an FP32 evaluator amendment, and the program text changed after attempt 4. I started seeds 0 and 1 only after reviewing seed 42. AP-Q comes from seed 0, which none of this touched.

\textbf{(ii) AutoResearch reference.} In the 50-attempt AutoResearch campaign, a cache-correctness check later found faulty discarded 27 trained candidates. AR-ref comes from a 5-attempt follow-up under a revised program. This is one reason AR-ref differs from our campaigns in more than the objective [-(\S\ref{sec:brief}).-]{+(\S\ref{sec:compare}).+}

\textbf{(iii) [-Pair-A-]{+Extended+} Codex arm.} Pod and provider failures forced requeues, the inference adapter was amended, the configuration changed before launch, and a later restart adjudicated failures inconsistently. We use this arm only as a second instance of the speed lever, never to rank agents.

\textbf{(iv) [-Pair-A-]{+Extended+} Opus arm.} A defect in the protected FP32 correctness adjudication was fixed mid-campaign and the affected attempt re-run. After three consecutive agent-call timeouts at attempt 9, I raised the call timeout from 600 to 900\,s for the rest of the arm (Codex ran at 600\,s). Attempt 9 was charged as a candidate failure, and timed-out calls made before any candidate were requeued. The arm was extended from 25 attempts to 25 valid results (27 attempts). Both arms moved to a second A40 once, Opus after A1 and Codex after A9. We also report the frozen first 25 attempts, and neither the timeout nor the extension changes a conclusion (Appendix~\ref{app:faq}).

\textbf{(v) Failure accounting.} The same tokenizer failure was counted differently in two campaigns. In seed 0 it consumed attempt A17, while in seed 42 it was relabelled as a setup failure. AP-Q's campaign thus carries the stricter accounting.

